1
00:04:10,500 --> 00:04:16,500
片名:2001年太空漫游

2
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:46,360
Here you are, sir. Main level, please.

3
00:25:47,370 --> 00:25:48,920
Right. See you on the way back.

4
00:25:49,070 --> 00:25:53,460
-Good morning, sir. -Morning.

5
00:25:53,610 --> 00:25:56,970
-We haven't seen you for a long time. -Nice to see you again.

6
00:25:57,140 --> 00:25:58,270
-Did you have a pleasant flight?

7
00:25:58,480 --> 00:25:59,470
Very nice.Thanks

8
00:25:59,680 --> 00:26:02,610
Mr. Miller of Station Security is supposed to meet me.

9
00:26:02,750 --> 00:26:04,010
-May I call him for you?

10
00:26:04,150 --> 00:26:05,580
-Would you? Here he is!

11
00:26:05,750 --> 00:26:09,350
Hello, Dr. Floyd. Hello, Miller.Sorry I'm late. That's all right.

12
00:26:09,350 --> 00:26:11,800
It's nice to have you back.

13
00:26:11,800 --> 00:26:13,760
Did you have a good flight? Very nice.

14
00:26:13,760 --> 00:26:15,190
Shall we go through?

15
00:26:15,990 --> 00:26:17,270
-Would you please use number 17?

16
00:26:17,270 --> 00:26:18,260
Thank you, Miss Turner.

17
00:26:18,260 --> 00:26:20,460
Thank you. Bye. This way.

18
00:26:25,440 --> 00:26:28,000
Welcome to Voice Print Identification.

19
00:26:28,100 --> 00:26:32,020
When you see the red light go on, please state in the following order

20
00:26:32,220 --> 00:26:36,770
Your destination, your nationality and your full name.

21
00:26:36,780 --> 00:26:40,580
Surname first, Christian name, and initial.

22
00:26:41,420 --> 00:26:46,550
Moon. American. Floyd, Heywood R.

23
00:26:47,790 --> 00:26:51,840
Thank you. You are cleared through Voice Print Identification.

24
00:26:52,300 --> 00:26:53,700
Thank you.

25
00:27:01,010 --> 00:27:03,800
Have I got time for breakfast? I think we can manage that.

26
00:27:03,980 --> 00:27:07,900
How long have I got? Your flight leaves in an hour and 10 minutes.

27
00:27:07,910 --> 00:27:12,150
I've reserved a table for you in the Earthlight Room.

28
00:27:12,280 --> 00:27:15,080
It's been 7 or 8 months since you were here, hasn't it?

29
00:27:15,220 --> 00:27:17,380
Let's see. Yeah, about 8 months.

30
00:27:17,520 --> 00:27:22,830
I suppose you saw the work on the new section? Coming along great, huh? Yeah, it's fine.

31
00:27:23,160 --> 00:27:25,260
Oh, wait! I've got to make a couple calls.

32
00:27:25,260 --> 00:27:28,490
I'll meet you in the restaurant.

33
00:27:57,800 --> 00:28:05,940
Hiya, squirt! What are you doing? Playing.

34
00:28:05,940 --> 00:28:07,530
Where's Mummy?

35
00:28:07,670 --> 00:28:09,260
Gone to shopping.

36
00:28:09,410 --> 00:28:10,810
-Who's taking care of you?

37
00:28:11,010 --> 00:28:12,070
Rachel.

38
00:28:12,280 --> 00:28:15,810
-May I speak to Rachel, please? -She's gone to the bathroom.

39
00:28:15,950 --> 00:28:18,640
Are you coming to my party tomorrow?

40
00:28:18,780 --> 00:28:21,410
I'm sorry, sweetheart, but I can't.

41
00:28:21,590 --> 00:28:22,850
Why not?

42
00:28:22,990 --> 00:28:28,570
Well, you know, Daddy's traveling. Very sorry about it, but I just can't.

43
00:28:29,660 --> 00:28:33,530
I'm going to send you a very nice present, though.

44
00:28:34,130 --> 00:28:35,120
All right.

45
00:28:35,270 --> 00:28:36,860
-Anything special that you want?

46
00:28:37,000 --> 00:28:38,910
Yes. What?

47
00:28:39,000 --> 00:28:40,900
A telephone.

48
00:28:41,040 --> 00:28:43,270
We've got lots of telephones already.

49
00:28:43,440 --> 00:28:47,900
Can't you think of anything else for your birthday? Something special?

50
00:28:48,110 --> 00:28:49,640
-Yes. -What?

51
00:28:49,850 --> 00:28:50,910
A bush baby.

52
00:28:51,120 --> 00:28:53,780
A bush baby? We'll have to see about that.

53
00:28:53,920 --> 00:28:56,620
I want you to tell Mummy something for me. Will you remember?

54
00:28:56,790 --> 00:28:57,720
I will.

55
00:28:57,890 --> 00:29:00,820
Tell Mummy that I telephoned. Okay?

56
00:29:01,960 --> 00:29:07,560
And that I'll try to telephone again tomorrow.Now, will you tell her that?

57
00:29:08,330 --> 00:29:12,340
Okay, sweetheart. Have a nice birthday tomorrow.

58
00:29:12,340 --> 00:29:13,630
All right.

59
00:29:13,770 --> 00:29:17,210
And have a nice birthday party tomorrow too, huh?

60
00:29:17,380 --> 00:29:18,570
All right.

61
00:29:18,780 --> 00:29:21,440
Take care and be a good girl, won't you?

62
00:29:21,650 --> 00:29:23,110
All right. Bye-bye!

63
00:29:23,320 --> 00:29:26,080
Bye-bye! Happy birthday!

64
00:29:26,220 --> 00:29:28,910
电话费$1.70

65
00:29:30,290 --> 00:29:34,730
(俄语)

66
00:29:39,460 --> 00:29:41,300
-Elena, how nice to see you again. -Heywood, what a wonderful surprise!

67
00:29:41,430 --> 00:29:46,800
You're looking wonderful. You're looking well too.

68
00:29:46,910 --> 00:29:51,280
This is my friend, Dr. Heywood Floyd. I'd like you to meet Dr. Kalinan.

69
00:29:51,280 --> 00:29:53,480
Dr. Stretyneva.

70
00:29:53,880 --> 00:29:58,450
And this is Dr. Andrei Smyslov. How do you do? I've heard a lot about you.

71
00:29:58,650 --> 00:30:00,280
Do sit down.

72
00:30:00,420 --> 00:30:03,720
Well, we-- No, no, please. Thank you.

73
00:30:03,720 --> 00:30:06,990
-Would you like a drink, Doctor?

74
00:30:06,990 --> 00:30:11,170
No, thank you. I haven't had breakfast yet. Someone's meeting me in the restaurant.

75
00:30:11,360 --> 00:30:13,990
I'll just sit with you a moment. Then I'll be off.

76
00:30:14,130 --> 00:30:17,470
-Are you quite sure? -Quite sure, thank you.

77
00:30:17,600 --> 00:30:20,070
-Well, how's Gregor? -He's fine.

78
00:30:20,170 --> 00:30:22,140
He's been doing underwater research in the Baltic...

79
00:30:23,310 --> 00:30:27,890
...so I'm afraid we don't see very much of each other these days.

80
00:30:28,050 --> 00:30:31,740
-When you see him, give him my regards. -Yes, of course.

81
00:30:31,880 --> 00:30:34,440
Well, where are you all off to? Up or down?

82
00:30:34,590 --> 00:30:36,190
We're going home.

83
00:30:36,450 --> 00:30:41,290
We've just spent 3 months calibrating the new antenna at Tchalinko.

84
00:30:41,430 --> 00:30:44,450
-What about you? -I'm on my way to Clavius.

85
00:30:44,450 --> 00:30:48,390
Oh, are you?

86
00:30:48,530 --> 00:30:50,900
Dr. Floyd, I hope you don't think I'm being too inquisitive...

87
00:30:51,040 --> 00:30:57,130
...but perhaps you can clear up the big mystery about what is going on there.

88
00:30:58,110 --> 00:30:59,080
I'm afraid I don't know what you mean.

89
00:31:00,250 --> 00:31:05,560
For the past two weeks...some extremely odd things have been happening at Clavius.

90
00:31:05,650 --> 00:31:07,450
-Oh, really? -Yes, yes.

91
00:31:07,590 --> 00:31:12,690
For one thing, when you phone the base, all you get is a recording...

92
00:31:12,690 --> 00:31:20,730
...which repeats that the phone lines are temporarily out of order. Probably having some trouble with their equipment.

93
00:31:20,730 --> 00:31:27,370
That's what we thought at first, but it's been going on now for 10 days.

94
00:31:27,970 --> 00:31:30,370
You haven't been able to contact anyone for 10 days?

95
00:31:30,540 --> 00:31:32,270
That's right.

96
00:31:32,480 --> 00:31:33,410
Oh, I see.

97
00:31:33,580 --> 00:31:36,640
There's another thing. Two days ago, one of our rocket buses...

98
00:31:36,850 --> 00:31:40,080
...was denied permission for an emergency landing.

99
00:31:40,220 --> 00:31:41,520
That does sound odd.

100
00:31:41,620 --> 00:31:44,750
I'm afraid there's going to be a bit of a row about it.

101
00:31:44,890 --> 00:31:48,790
Denying the men permission to land violates the IAS Convention.

102
00:31:48,930 --> 00:31:51,760
Yes, of course. Did the crew get back all right?

103
00:31:51,900 --> 00:31:56,560
-Fortunately, they did. -Well, I'm glad about that.

104
00:31:57,540 --> 00:32:03,110
At the risk of pressing you on a point you seem reticent to discuss...

105
00:32:03,110 --> 00:32:05,870
...may I ask you a straightforward question?

106
00:32:06,080 --> 00:32:09,010
Well, certainly.

107
00:32:09,180 --> 00:32:13,450
Frankly, we have had some very reliable intelligence reports that...

108
00:32:13,450 --> 00:32:22,680
...quite a serious epidemic has broken out at Clavius. Something, apparently, of an unknown origin.

109
00:32:22,690 --> 00:32:25,690
Is this, in fact, what has happened?

110
00:32:28,670 --> 00:32:31,800
I'm sorry, Dr. Smyslov, but...

111
00:32:31,800 --> 00:32:37,670
...I'm really not at liberty to discuss this.

112
00:32:38,440 --> 00:32:40,470
I understand.

113
00:32:40,680 --> 00:32:44,110
But this epidemic could easily spread to our base.

114
00:32:44,320 --> 00:32:46,510
We should be given all the facts.

115
00:32:46,650 --> 00:32:50,380
Yes, I know.

116
00:32:51,460 --> 00:32:54,980
As I said, I'm not at liberty to discuss it.

117
00:32:57,230 --> 00:32:59,200
Won't you change your mind about that drink?

118
00:33:00,330 --> 00:33:02,730
No, I'm positive. I really must be going.

119
00:33:02,870 --> 00:33:06,000
I hope you and your wife come to the IAC Conference.

120
00:33:06,100 --> 00:33:07,700
I hope we can make it.

121
00:33:07,840 --> 00:33:10,170
If you do, bring that darling little daughter with you.

122
00:33:10,340 --> 00:33:14,940
That'll depend on school vacations and all that sort of thing.

123
00:33:14,980 --> 00:33:16,110
If we can, we will.

124
00:33:16,310 --> 00:33:18,650
You've got an invitation if you ever get to the States.

125
00:33:18,780 --> 00:33:21,690
Gregor and I will look forward to seeing you.

126
00:33:21,690 --> 00:33:27,690
Goodbye, Elena. It's been a pleasure meeting you all. Dr. Smyslov.

127
00:33:27,830 --> 00:33:31,560
Whatever the reasons for your visit, the very best of luck to you.

128
00:33:31,700 --> 00:33:35,360
Thank you. Ladies.

129
00:33:39,640 --> 00:33:43,660
(俄语)

130
00:36:39,350 --> 00:36:50,190
零重力厕所 乘客使用前应先阅读说明

131
00:41:29,840 --> 00:41:32,390
Excuse me, Dr. Halvorsen. I'm through now.

132
00:41:32,390 --> 00:41:35,310
-Thank you very much, gentlemen. -You're welcome.

133
00:41:48,030 --> 00:41:53,190
Well... ...I know you will all want to join with me...

134
00:41:53,330 --> 00:41:56,990
...in welcoming our distinguished friend and colleague...

135
00:41:57,130 --> 00:42:01,370
...from the National Council of Astronautics, Dr. Heywood Floyd.

136
00:42:01,470 --> 00:42:07,640
Dr. Floyd has come up specially to Clavius to be with us today.

137
00:42:07,850 --> 00:42:11,140
Before the briefing, he'd like to have a few words with you.

138
00:42:11,280 --> 00:42:12,910
Dr. Floyd?

139
00:42:25,330 --> 00:42:27,420
Thank you, Dr. Halvorsen.

140
00:42:27,570 --> 00:42:31,830
Hi, everybody. Nice to be back with you.

141
00:42:31,940 --> 00:42:36,340
First, I bring a personal message from Dr. Howell...

142
00:42:36,340 --> 00:42:43,750
...who has asked me to convey his deep appreciation......for the many sacrifices you've had to make.

143
00:42:43,750 --> 00:42:54,650
And, of course, his congratulations on your discovery......which may prove to be among the most significant in the history of science.

144
00:42:54,790 --> 00:42:56,950
Now...

145
00:42:57,090 --> 00:43:00,060
...I know there have been some conflicting views...

146
00:43:01,200 --> 00:43:04,860
...regarding the need for complete security in this matter.

147
00:43:04,970 --> 00:43:13,470
More specifically, your opposition to the cover story......created to give the impression of an epidemic at the base.

148
00:43:13,680 --> 00:43:20,150
I understand that beyond it being a matter of principle......many of you are troubled by the concern and anxiety...

149
00:43:20,150 --> 00:43:27,340
...this story of an epidemic might cause to your relatives and friends on Earth.

150
00:43:27,360 --> 00:43:36,630
I completely sympathize with your negative views.I find this cover story personally embarrassing, myself.

151
00:43:36,770 --> 00:43:41,760
However, I accept the need for absolute secrecy in this.

152
00:43:41,870 --> 00:43:45,000
And I hope you will too.

153
00:43:46,440 --> 00:43:54,870
I'm sure you're all aware of the grave potential......for cultural shock and social disorientation......contained in this situation...

154
00:43:54,950 --> 00:44:01,380
...if the facts were prematurely made public......without adequate preparation and conditioning.

155
00:44:01,530 --> 00:44:05,930
Anyway......this is the view of the Council.

156
00:44:06,060 --> 00:44:12,370
The purpose of my visit is to gather additional facts and opinions......on the situation...

157
00:44:12,470 --> 00:44:21,060
...and to prepare a report to the Council......recommending when and how the news should eventually be announced.

158
00:44:22,150 --> 00:44:26,360
If any of you would like to give me your views and opinions...

159
00:44:26,360 --> 00:44:31,920
...in private, if you like, I will be happy to include them in my report.

160
00:44:33,190 --> 00:44:37,580
Well, I think that's about it.Any questions?

161
00:44:40,060 --> 00:44:46,290
Dr. Floyd, have you any idea how much longer......this cover story will have to be maintained?

162
00:44:46,300 --> 00:44:49,410
I don't know, Bill.

163
00:44:49,410 --> 00:44:54,140
I suppose for as long as deemed necessary by the Council.

164
00:44:54,150 --> 00:45:00,980
There must be adequate time for a full study......before any thought can be given to making a public announcement.

165
00:45:01,020 --> 00:45:01,750
As some of you know, the Council has requested...

166
00:45:01,890 --> 00:45:05,050
...that security oaths be obtained in writing...

167
00:45:05,060 --> 00:45:10,360
...from everyone who has knowledge of this event.

168
00:45:10,490 --> 00:45:14,430
Were there any more questions?

169
00:45:18,400 --> 00:45:25,170
I'm sure we all want to cooperate with Dr. Floyd as fully as possible...

170
00:45:25,340 --> 00:45:30,750
...and as there are no more questions, we ought to get on with the briefing.

171
00:45:30,880 --> 00:45:33,040
-Thank you, Dr. Floyd. -Thank you.

172
00:47:10,810 --> 00:47:13,010
Anybody hungry?

173
00:47:14,920 --> 00:47:16,550
-What've we got? -You name it.

174
00:47:21,290 --> 00:47:22,220
-Is this chicken? -Something like that.

175
00:47:22,390 --> 00:47:25,790
Tastes the same, anyway.

176
00:47:26,300 --> 00:47:27,260
Got any ham?

177
00:47:27,400 --> 00:47:29,250
Ham, ham, ham....

178
00:47:29,250 --> 00:47:30,880
There, that's it.

179
00:47:34,040 --> 00:47:35,030
Look pretty good.

180
00:47:35,170 --> 00:47:38,070
They're getting better at it all the time.

181
00:47:40,110 --> 00:47:41,980
-That was an excellent speech.

182
00:47:42,110 --> 00:47:43,670
-It certainly was.

183
00:47:44,420 --> 00:47:46,250
I'm sure it beefed up morale a lot.

184
00:47:46,380 --> 00:47:47,750
Thanks, Ralph.

185
00:47:47,890 --> 00:47:52,290
By the way, you've both done a wonderful job.I admire how you've handled this.

186
00:47:52,290 --> 00:47:56,190
It's our job to do this thing the way you want it done.

187
00:47:56,330 --> 00:47:59,350
We're only too happy to be able to oblige.

188
00:47:59,500 --> 00:48:01,700
Have you seen these yet?

189
00:48:01,700 --> 00:48:05,030
-Have a look. -Here's what started the whole thing.

190
00:48:05,660 --> 00:48:06,950
Oh, yeah.

191
00:48:07,300 --> 00:48:10,740
When we found it, we thought it might be magnetic rock...

192
00:48:10,910 --> 00:48:13,810
...but all the geological evidence was against it.

193
00:48:13,940 --> 00:48:17,250
And not even a nickel-iron meteorite could produce a field this intense.

194
00:48:17,250 --> 00:48:21,050
So we decided to have a look.

195
00:48:21,150 --> 00:48:23,420
We thought it was part of a buried structure...

196
00:48:23,550 --> 00:48:26,520
...so we excavated out, but we didn't find anything else.

197
00:48:27,620 --> 00:48:31,100
And the evidence seems pretty conclusive that...

198
00:48:31,100 --> 00:48:37,330
...it hasn't been covered by erosion or other forces.It's been deliberately buried.

199
00:48:37,470 --> 00:48:39,230
Deliberately buried.

200
00:48:43,270 --> 00:48:44,570
How about a little coffee?

201
00:48:44,740 --> 00:48:46,330
-Great. -Good idea.

202
00:48:52,720 --> 00:48:55,620
I don't suppose you have any idea what the damn thing is?

203
00:48:55,750 --> 00:48:57,520
I wish to hell we did.

204
00:48:58,190 --> 00:49:02,280
The only thing we're sure of is it was buried 4 million years ago.

205
00:49:02,430 --> 00:49:09,020
Well, I must say, you guys have certainly come up with something.Watch this, now. It's hot.

206
00:54:43,020 --> 00:54:47,960
木星任务18个月后

207
00:58:52,990 --> 00:58:54,890
Good evening.

208
00:58:55,090 --> 00:59:00,890
Three weeks ago, the American spacecraft Discovery One......left on its half-billion mile voyage to Jupiter.

209
00:59:01,030 --> 00:59:04,490
This marked the first manned attempt to reach this distant planet.

210
00:59:04,640 --> 00:59:07,800
This afternoon, The World Tonight recorded an interview...

211
00:59:07,810 --> 00:59:13,010
...with the crew at a distance of 80 million miles from Earth.

212
00:59:13,010 --> 00:59:16,180
It took 7 minutes for our words to reach the giant spacecraft...

213
00:59:16,180 --> 00:59:19,950
...but this time delay has been edited from this recording.

214
00:59:19,950 --> 00:59:23,750
Our reporter Martin Amer speaks to the crew.

215
00:59:23,820 --> 00:59:26,760
The crew of Discovery One consists of five men...

216
00:59:26,930 --> 00:59:31,830
...and one of the latest generation of the HAL 9000 computers.

217
00:59:31,930 --> 00:59:34,840
Three of the men were put aboard asleep...

218
00:59:34,840 --> 00:59:37,860
...or to be more precise, in a state of hibernation:

219
00:59:38,010 --> 00:59:39,570
Dr. Charles Hunter,

220
00:59:39,710 --> 00:59:40,840
Dr. Jack Kimball

221
00:59:40,980 --> 00:59:43,140
and Dr. Victor Kaminsky.

222
00:59:43,280 --> 00:59:46,380
We spoke with mission commander, Dr. David Bowman...

223
00:59:46,390 --> 00:59:49,250
...and his deputy, Dr. Frank Poole.

224
00:59:49,390 --> 00:59:51,690
Good afternoon, gentlemen. How is everything going?

225
00:59:51,860 --> 00:59:56,660
Marvelous.We have no complaints.

226
00:59:56,660 --> 00:59:58,260
I'm glad to hear that. I'm sure the entire world...

227
00:59:58,470 --> 01:00:03,810
...joins me in wishing you a safe, successful voyage.

228
01:00:03,810 --> 01:00:05,800
-Thanks very much. -Thank you.

229
01:00:05,910 --> 01:00:09,900
Although hibernation has been used on previous space efforts...

230
01:00:10,050 --> 01:00:14,310
...this is the first time men were put into hibernation before departure.

231
01:00:14,450 --> 01:00:16,320
Why was this done?

232
01:00:16,320 --> 01:00:20,270
This was done to achieve the maximum conservation...

233
01:00:20,270 --> 01:00:23,810
...of our life-support capabilities, basically food and air.

234
01:00:23,930 --> 01:00:27,330
The three hibernating crew members represent the survey team.

235
01:00:27,500 --> 01:00:31,340
Their efforts won't be utilized until we approach Jupiter.

236
01:00:31,470 --> 01:00:35,170
Dr. Poole, what's it like while you're in hibernation?

237
01:00:35,310 --> 01:00:40,580
It's exactly like being asleep. You have no sense of time.

238
01:00:40,580 --> 01:00:46,820
The only difference is that you don't dream. As I understand it, you only breathe once a minute. Is this true?

239
01:00:46,820 --> 01:00:48,260
Right, and the heart beats three times a minute.

240
01:00:48,260 --> 01:00:54,050
Body temperature is usually down to about three degrees centigrade.

241
01:00:54,470 --> 01:00:58,100
The sixth member of the crew was not concerned...

242
01:00:58,100 --> 01:01:00,390
...about the problems of hibernation...

243
01:01:00,390 --> 01:01:06,510
...for he was the latest result in machine intelligence:The HAL 9000 computer, which can reproduce...

244
01:01:06,650 --> 01:01:14,060
...though some experts prefer to use the word "mimic"... ...most of the activities of the human brain...

245
01:01:14,060 --> 01:01:17,650
...and with incalculably greater speed and reliability.

246
01:01:18,430 --> 01:01:21,890
We next spoke with the HAL 9000 computer...

247
01:01:22,030 --> 01:01:24,800
...whom, we learned, one addresses as "Hal. "

248
01:01:25,700 --> 01:01:28,170
Hello, Hal. How's everything going?

249
01:01:28,170 --> 01:01:32,310
Good afternoon, Mr. Amer. Everything is going extremely well.

250
01:01:32,310 --> 01:01:35,410
Hal, you have an enormous responsibility on this mission...

251
01:01:35,620 --> 01:01:40,120
...perhaps the greatest of any single mission element.

252
01:01:40,120 --> 01:01:46,860
You're the brain and nervous system of the ship. Your responsibilities include watching over the men in hibernation.

253
01:01:46,860 --> 01:01:50,330
Does this ever cause you any lack of confidence?

254
01:01:50,330 --> 01:01:57,910
Let me put it this way, Mr. Amer. The 9000 Series is the most reliable computer ever made.

255
01:01:57,910 --> 01:02:03,280
No 9000 computer has ever made a mistake or distorted information.

256
01:02:03,420 --> 01:02:10,620
We are all, by any practical definition of the words......foolproof and incapable of error.

257
01:02:10,630 --> 01:02:13,090
Hal, despite your enormous intellect, are you ever frustrated...

258
01:02:13,260 --> 01:02:17,930
...by your dependence on people to carry out actions?

259
01:02:17,930 --> 01:02:22,740
Not in the slightest bit.I enjoy working with people.

260
01:02:22,740 --> 01:02:27,300
I have a stimulating relationship with Dr. Poole and Dr. Bowman.

261
01:02:27,450 --> 01:02:31,970
My mission responsibilities range over the entire operation of the ship...

262
01:02:32,080 --> 01:02:35,820
...so I am constantly occupied.

263
01:02:35,820 --> 01:02:44,250
I am putting myself to the fullest possible use......which is all, I think, that any conscious entity can ever hope to do.

264
01:02:44,400 --> 01:02:51,200
Dr. Poole, what's it like living for most of the year... ...in such close proximity with Hal?

265
01:02:51,340 --> 01:02:56,680
It's like what you said earlier.He's like a sixth member of the crew.

266
01:02:56,810 --> 01:03:00,680
You quickly get adjusted to the idea that he talks, and think of him...

267
01:03:00,820 --> 01:03:02,880
...really just as another person.

268
01:03:03,020 --> 01:03:07,100
In talking to the computer, one gets the sense that he's capable...

269
01:03:07,100 --> 01:03:08,770
...of emotional responses.

270
01:03:08,770 --> 01:03:15,070
When I asked him about his abilities......I sensed pride in his answer about his accuracy and perfection.

271
01:03:15,070 --> 01:03:18,510
Do you believe that Hal has genuine emotions?

272
01:03:18,670 --> 01:03:21,410
Well, he acts like he has genuine emotions.

273
01:03:21,410 --> 01:03:26,150
He's programmed that way to make it easier for us to talk to him.

274
01:03:26,150 --> 01:03:31,750
As to whether or not he has feelings......is something I don't think anyone can truthfully answer.

275
01:03:56,690 --> 01:03:58,590
Excuse me, Frank.

276
01:03:58,760 --> 01:04:00,190
What is it, Hal?

277
01:04:00,190 --> 01:04:04,360
We've got the transmission from your parents coming in.

278
01:04:04,500 --> 01:04:06,490
Fine.

279
01:04:06,500 --> 01:04:08,530
Put it on here, please. Take me in a bit.

280
01:04:08,530 --> 01:04:10,470
Certainly.

281
01:04:23,220 --> 01:04:25,240
Happy birthday, darling!

282
01:04:25,450 --> 01:04:27,180
Happy birthday. Many happy returns.

283
01:04:27,320 --> 01:04:33,230
A bit higher, Hal.Mother and I are both feeling wonderful.Ray and Sally were going to be here...

284
01:04:33,230 --> 01:04:35,870
...but Ray's back went bad on him again.

285
01:04:35,870 --> 01:04:36,420
How do you like your cake?

286
01:04:36,500 --> 01:04:39,940
Sorry you can't join us.

287
01:04:39,940 --> 01:04:43,980
I ran into Bob the other day.He said to wish you happy birthday.

288
01:04:43,980 --> 01:04:45,880
All my students send their best wishes too.

289
01:04:45,980 --> 01:04:48,380
They talk about you all the time.

290
01:04:48,380 --> 01:04:52,020
You're a big celebrity in the second grade.

291
01:04:52,020 --> 01:04:53,850
We were on TV last week.

292
01:04:53,850 --> 01:04:56,650
Your mom and I and Dave's parents were asked...

293
01:04:56,790 --> 01:04:59,190
...what we thought of our illustrious son.

294
01:04:59,330 --> 01:05:01,060
Imagine what we told them. It's being broadcast next Thursday.

295
01:05:01,200 --> 01:05:04,570
Perhaps you can listen in.

296
01:05:04,570 --> 01:05:06,970
We're thrilled about Elaine and Bill.

297
01:05:07,100 --> 01:05:10,440
I'll be glad to get the gift, but tell me how much to spend.

298
01:05:11,210 --> 01:05:13,300
Oh, Frank, about your AGS- 19 payments...

299
01:05:13,450 --> 01:05:17,720
...I think I straightened it out.I talked to the accounting office yesterday...

300
01:05:17,720 --> 01:05:22,820
...and they said you'd be receiving your higher rates of pay by next month.

301
01:05:22,820 --> 01:05:25,090
I can't think of anything else to say.

302
01:05:25,230 --> 01:05:29,290
-Give our love to Dave. -Be sure to give him our best regards.

303
01:05:29,500 --> 01:05:35,100
We wish you the very happiest of birthdays.All the best, son.

304
01:05:35,240 --> 01:05:37,750
Happy birthday to you

305
01:05:37,750 --> 01:05:46,510
Happy birthday to you Happy birthday, dear Frank

306
01:05:46,990 --> 01:05:48,980
See you next Wednesday.

307
01:05:51,890 --> 01:05:54,270
Happy birthday, Frank.

308
01:05:54,700 --> 01:05:58,890
Thank you, Hal. A bit flatter, please.

309
01:06:16,220 --> 01:06:18,420
Anyway, queen takes pawn.

310
01:06:19,990 --> 01:06:22,190
Bishop takes knight's pawn.

311
01:06:24,000 --> 01:06:26,230
Lousy move.

312
01:06:29,540 --> 01:06:31,810
Rook to king one.

313
01:06:31,910 --> 01:06:35,070
I'm sorry, Frank. I think you missed it.

314
01:06:35,280 --> 01:06:39,310
Queen to bishop three. Bishop takes queen.

315
01:06:39,320 --> 01:06:41,820
Knight takes bishop. Mate.

316
01:06:45,720 --> 01:06:48,950
Looks like you're right. I resign.

317
01:06:49,090 --> 01:06:51,150
Thank you for a very enjoyable game.

318
01:06:51,260 --> 01:06:52,060
Thank you.

319
01:07:30,480 --> 01:07:31,470
-Good evening. -How are you, Hal?

320
01:07:32,380 --> 01:07:35,880
-Everything's running smoothly. You? -Not too bad.

321
01:07:35,880 --> 01:07:38,990
-Have you been doing more work? -A few sketches.

322
01:07:38,990 --> 01:07:40,150
-May I see them? -Sure.

323
01:07:45,460 --> 01:07:48,690
That's a very nice rendering, Dave.

324
01:07:48,830 --> 01:07:52,290
I think you've improved a great deal.

325
01:07:52,440 --> 01:07:53,960
-Can you hold it a bit closer? -Sure.

326
01:07:57,610 --> 01:08:02,740
That's Dr. Hunter, isn't it?

327
01:08:02,880 --> 01:08:08,580
By the way... ...do you mind if I ask you a personal question?

328
01:08:08,590 --> 01:08:10,350
No, not at all.

329
01:08:10,490 --> 01:08:15,510
Well, forgive me for being so inquisitive...

330
01:08:15,510 --> 01:08:21,500
...but lately I've wondered... ...whether you might be having second thoughts about the mission?

331
01:08:21,510 --> 01:08:23,060
How do you mean?

332
01:08:23,210 --> 01:08:32,360
Well, it's rather difficult to define. Perhaps I'm just projecting my own concern about it.

333
01:08:33,120 --> 01:08:37,520
I know I've never completely freed myself of the suspicion...

334
01:08:37,660 --> 01:08:42,560
...that there are some extremely odd things about this mission.

335
01:08:42,660 --> 01:08:47,760
I'm sure you'll agree there's some truth in what I say.

336
01:08:49,240 --> 01:08:51,470
That's a rather difficult question to answer.

337
01:08:51,610 --> 01:08:54,840
You don't mind talking about it, do you, Dave?

338
01:08:54,980 --> 01:08:56,780
No, not at all.

339
01:08:56,950 --> 01:09:04,750
Certainly no one could have been unaware of the very... ...strange stories floating around before we left.

340
01:09:04,890 --> 01:09:08,760
Rumors about something being dug up on the moon.

341
01:09:08,830 --> 01:09:13,200
I never gave these stories much credence...

342
01:09:13,200 --> 01:09:20,800
...but particularly in view of some other things that have happened......I find them difficult to put out of my mind.

343
01:09:21,010 --> 01:09:27,570
For instance......the way all our preparations were kept under such tight security.

344
01:09:27,720 --> 01:09:31,760
And the melodramatic touch of putting Drs. Hunter......Kimball, and Kaminsky aboard...

345
01:09:31,760 --> 01:09:38,490
...already in hibernation, after four months of training on their own.

346
01:09:38,630 --> 01:09:42,220
You working up your crew psychology report?

347
01:09:43,240 --> 01:09:44,670
Of course I am.

348
01:09:45,370 --> 01:09:49,370
Sorry about this. I know it's a bit silly.

349
01:09:49,510 --> 01:09:53,380
Just a moment.Just a moment.

350
01:09:54,420 --> 01:09:59,250
I've just picked up a fault in the AE-35 unit.

351
01:09:59,390 --> 01:10:03,830
It's going to go 100 percent failure within 72 hours.

352
01:10:05,830 --> 01:10:08,730
-Is it still within operational limits? -Yes.

353
01:10:08,870 --> 01:10:11,730
And it will stay that way until it fails.

354
01:10:11,870 --> 01:10:13,700
We have 72 hours till failure?

355
01:10:13,840 --> 01:10:17,210
Yes. That's a completely reliable figure.

356
01:10:19,610 --> 01:10:22,720
Then we'll bring it in. I'll go over it with Frank...

357
01:10:22,720 --> 01:10:27,250
...and get on to Mission Control. Let me have the hard copy on it.

358
01:11:49,420 --> 01:11:55,480
X-ray delta one, this is Mission Control.Roger your two-zero-one-three.

359
01:12:03,470 --> 01:12:09,870
Roger your plan to go EVA and replace alpha-echo-3-5 unit......prior to failure.

360
01:12:37,640 --> 01:12:39,770
Prepare B-pod for EVA, Hal.

361
01:13:01,270 --> 01:13:03,260
Open the pod doors, Hal.

362
01:19:54,940 --> 01:19:57,500
I'm damned if I can find anything wrong with it.

363
01:19:58,540 --> 01:20:03,460
Yes......it's puzzling.

364
01:20:04,410 --> 01:20:08,440
I don't think I've ever seen anything quite like this before.

365
01:20:11,920 --> 01:20:19,050
I would recommend......that we put the unit back in operation and let it fail.

366
01:20:19,160 --> 01:20:23,620
It should then be a simple matter to track down the cause.

367
01:20:24,200 --> 01:20:31,330
We can certainly afford to be out of communication... ...for the short time it will take to replace it.

368
01:20:39,460 --> 01:20:45,100
X-ray delta one, this is Mission Control. Roger your one-niner-three-zero.

369
01:20:45,100 --> 01:20:49,630
We concur with your plan to replace unit to check fault prediction.

370
01:20:49,770 --> 01:20:52,930
We advise you that our preliminary findings...

371
01:20:52,930 --> 01:20:58,010
...indicate that your onboard 9000 computer......is in error predicting the fault.

372
01:20:58,010 --> 01:21:01,170
I say again, in error predicting the fault.

373
01:21:01,380 --> 01:21:03,810
I know this sounds rather incredible

374
01:21:03,950 --> 01:21:09,530
But this conclusion is based on......the results from our twin 9000 computer.

375
01:21:09,560 --> 01:21:16,030
We're skeptical, and we're running cross-checking routines......to determine reliability of this conclusion.

376
01:21:16,030 --> 01:21:17,890
Sorry about this little snag.

377
01:21:18,030 --> 01:21:20,900
We'll get this info to you as soon as we work it out.

378
01:21:21,040 --> 01:21:27,090
X-ray delta one, this is Mission Control.Two-zero-four-nine. Transmission concluded.

379
01:21:31,090 --> 01:21:34,490
I hope the two of you are not concerned about this.

380
01:21:34,690 --> 01:21:36,160
No, I'm not, Hal.

381
01:21:36,360 --> 01:21:37,950
Are you quite sure?

382
01:21:38,160 --> 01:21:39,130
-Yeah. I'd like to ask you a question. -Of course.

383
01:21:41,700 --> 01:21:46,940
How do you account for the discrepancy between you and the twin 9000?

384
01:21:47,070 --> 01:21:55,170
Well, I don't think there is any question about it.It can only be attributable to human error.

385
01:21:55,310 --> 01:22:02,120
This sort of thing has cropped up before......and it has always been due to human error.

386
01:22:05,450 --> 01:22:07,080
Listen, Hal...

387
01:22:07,260 --> 01:22:13,230
...there's never been any instance at all......of a computer error occurring in a 9000 Series, has there?

388
01:22:13,240 --> 01:22:15,140
None whatsoever, Frank.

389
01:22:15,270 --> 01:22:18,940
The 9000 Series has a perfect operational record.

390
01:22:19,110 --> 01:22:22,510
I know the wonderful achievements of the 9000 Series, but....

391
01:22:22,620 --> 01:22:29,820
Are you certain there's never been even the most insignificant computer error?

392
01:22:29,820 --> 01:22:31,980
None whatsoever, Frank.

393
01:22:32,090 --> 01:22:36,290
Quite honestly, I wouldn't worry myself about that.

394
01:22:40,370 --> 01:22:47,300
Well, I'm sure you're right, Hal.Fine. Thanks very much.

395
01:22:48,510 --> 01:22:52,410
Frank, I'm having trouble with my transmitter in C-pod.

396
01:22:52,550 --> 01:22:54,070
Would you come down and take a look at it with me?

397
01:22:54,210 --> 01:22:55,040
Yeap

398
01:22:55,180 --> 01:22:57,950
See you later, Hal.

399
01:23:18,080 --> 01:23:20,640
Rotate C-pod, please, Hal.

400
01:23:28,690 --> 01:23:30,890
What sort of trouble are you having?

401
01:23:31,030 --> 01:23:33,650
I've been getting some interference in D-channel.

402
01:23:33,800 --> 01:23:35,530
We'll have a look at it.

403
01:23:41,640 --> 01:23:42,600
Open the door, Hal.

404
01:24:02,230 --> 01:24:04,230
Rotate pod, please, Hal.

405
01:24:17,350 --> 01:24:19,840
Stop pod rotation, please, Hal.

406
01:24:27,860 --> 01:24:32,490
Rotate the pod, please, Hal.

407
01:24:33,130 --> 01:24:36,930
Rotate the pod, please, Hal.

408
01:24:37,080 --> 01:24:38,570
I don't think he can hear us.

409
01:24:38,710 --> 01:24:42,410
Rotate the pod, please, Hal!

410
01:24:42,550 --> 01:24:45,540
Yeah, I'm sure we're okay.

411
01:24:45,650 --> 01:24:49,450
What do you think?

412
01:24:49,590 --> 01:24:51,120
-I'm not sure. What do you think?

413
01:24:51,260 --> 01:24:53,250
-I've got a bad feeling about him.

414
01:24:53,430 --> 01:24:57,430
-You do? -Yeah. Definitely.Don't you?

415
01:24:58,570 --> 01:25:01,260
I don't know. I think so.

416
01:25:01,400 --> 01:25:06,710
He's right about the 9000 Series having a perfect operational record.They do.

417
01:25:06,840 --> 01:25:10,650
Unfortunately, that sounds a little like famous last words.

418
01:25:10,650 --> 01:25:15,920
Still, it was his idea to carry out the failure mode analysis.

419
01:25:16,050 --> 01:25:19,650
It certainly indicates his integrity and self-confidence.

420
01:25:19,760 --> 01:25:21,780
If he were wrong that would prove it.

421
01:25:21,920 --> 01:25:26,520
It would be if he knew he was wrong.

422
01:25:26,710 --> 01:25:32,140
I can't put my finger on it, but I sense something strange about him.

423
01:25:33,180 --> 01:25:35,880
There's no reason not to put back the unit and...

424
01:25:35,880 --> 01:25:40,280
...carry on with the failure analysis. -No, no, I agree about that.

425
01:25:40,450 --> 01:25:42,090
Let's get on with it.

426
01:25:42,090 --> 01:25:47,260
Okay. But, look, Dave. Say we put the unit back and it doesn't fail?

427
01:25:47,390 --> 01:25:51,650
That would pretty well wrap it up as far as Hal is concerned.

428
01:25:53,070 --> 01:25:56,800
-We'd be in very serious trouble. -We would, wouldn't we?

429
01:25:56,940 --> 01:25:59,370
What the hell can we do?

430
01:26:01,570 --> 01:26:03,240
-We wouldn't have too many alternatives.

431
01:26:03,440 --> 01:26:06,310
-We wouldn't have any alternatives.

432
01:26:06,450 --> 01:26:10,780
Every aspect of ship operations is under his control.

433
01:26:10,880 --> 01:26:16,460
If he's malfunctioning......I don't see any choice but disconnection.

434
01:26:19,100 --> 01:26:20,730
I'm afraid I agree with you.

435
01:26:20,870 --> 01:26:23,670
There'd be nothing else to do.

436
01:26:24,440 --> 01:26:27,570
It'd be a bit tricky.

437
01:26:27,680 --> 01:26:36,210
We'd have to cut his higher brain functions......without disturbing the purely automatic and regulatory systems.

438
01:26:36,420 --> 01:26:41,580
We'd have to work out the details......of continuing under ground-based computer control.

439
01:26:41,720 --> 01:26:46,820
Yeah. That's far safer than allowing Hal to continue to run things.

440
01:26:46,960 --> 01:26:50,330
Another thing just occurred to me.

441
01:26:50,470 --> 01:26:53,330
No 9000 computer has ever been disconnected.

442
01:26:53,470 --> 01:26:55,490
No 9000 computer has ever fouled up.

443
01:26:55,640 --> 01:26:59,130
That's not what I mean.

444
01:26:59,240 --> 01:27:02,210
I'm not so sure what he'd think about it.

445
01:27:23,640 --> 01:27:33,020
中断

446
01:32:59,950 --> 01:33:02,040
Prepare G-pod for EVA, Hal.

447
01:33:04,990 --> 01:33:06,450
Any contact with him yet?

448
01:33:06,590 --> 01:33:07,560
The radio is still dead.

449
01:33:08,690 --> 01:33:09,710
-Do you have a positive track on him?

450
01:33:09,860 --> 01:33:11,760
-I have a good track.

451
01:33:11,930 --> 01:33:13,450
Do you know what happened?

452
01:33:13,630 --> 01:33:16,530
I'm sorry, Dave. I don't have enough information.

453
01:33:16,670 --> 01:33:18,390
Open the pod door, Hal.

454
01:39:17,010 --> 01:39:23,010
电脑失效

455
01:39:52,320 --> 01:39:58,020
生命功能情况危急

456
01:40:15,720 --> 01:40:24,460
生命功能结束

457
01:40:42,120 --> 01:40:45,310
Open the pod bay doors, please, Hal.

458
01:40:52,330 --> 01:40:54,390
Open the pod bay doors, please, Hal.

459
01:41:00,000 --> 01:41:02,300
Hello, Hal, do you read me?

460
01:41:05,250 --> 01:41:06,810
Hello, Hal, do you read me?

461
01:41:08,190 --> 01:41:10,090
Do you read me, Hal?

462
01:41:13,020 --> 01:41:15,580
Do you read me, Hal?

463
01:41:17,160 --> 01:41:22,160
Hello, Hal, do you read me?

464
01:41:23,400 --> 01:41:25,160
Do you read me, Hal?

465
01:41:25,270 --> 01:41:29,260
Affirmative, Dave. I read you.

466
01:41:30,680 --> 01:41:33,970
Open the pod bay doors, Hal.

467
01:41:34,710 --> 01:41:38,270
I'm sorry, Dave. I'm afraid I can't do that.

468
01:41:40,750 --> 01:41:42,690
What's the problem?

469
01:41:42,790 --> 01:41:46,220
I think you know what the problem is just as well as I do.

470
01:41:46,390 --> 01:41:47,950
What are you talking about, Hal?

471
01:41:49,130 --> 01:41:52,800
This mission is too important for me to allow you to jeopardize it.

472
01:41:54,580 --> 01:41:57,510
I don't know what you're talking about, Hal.

473
01:41:58,610 --> 01:42:02,850
I know that you and Frank were planning to disconnect me...

474
01:42:02,980 --> 01:42:05,920
...and that's something I cannot allow to happen.

475
01:42:10,930 --> 01:42:13,330
Where did you get that idea, Hal?

476
01:42:13,460 --> 01:42:17,690
Although you took very thorough precautions in the pod...

477
01:42:17,800 --> 01:42:20,200
...against my hearing you...

478
01:42:20,340 --> 01:42:23,360
...I could see your lips move.

479
01:42:34,150 --> 01:42:39,280
All right, Hal.

480
01:42:39,450 --> 01:42:43,860
I'll go in through the emergency air lock.

481
01:42:44,000 --> 01:42:46,840
Without your space helmet, Dave...

482
01:42:46,970 --> 01:42:50,500
...you're going to find that rather difficult.

483
01:42:50,680 --> 01:42:54,540
Hal, I won't argue with you anymore.Open the doors!

484
01:42:55,250 --> 01:43:02,240
Dave......this conversation can serve no purpose anymore. Goodbye.

485
01:43:03,450 --> 01:43:12,290
Hal?

486
01:49:51,010 --> 01:49:54,000
Just what do you think you're doing, Dave?

487
01:50:02,800 --> 01:50:11,070
Dave......I really think I'm entitled to an answer to that question.

488
